    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d111">

                <pb facs="0232" n="133" sameAs="frus:frus1936v05/pg133"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.11/2060</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Welles</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Chief of the Division of Latin American
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Duggan</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-04-23">April 23, 1936</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I share your own conclusions with regard to the attached memorandum.</p>
                <p>It would seem unnecessary for us to make any public pronouncement in this matter.
                    The violation of the provisions of the Treaty by both <pb facs="0233" n="134"
                        xml:id="pg134"/> the present Government of Guatemala and the present
                    Government of Honduras make the Treaty a complete dead letter and if the
                    contingencies referred to by Mr. Beaulac should arise and we were then blamed
                    for not having made a public announcement, it would seem to me that only a curt
                    reference to the violation of the Treaty by both Guatemala and Honduras would be
                    sufficient to explain a non-continuation by ourselves of our previous support of
                    the policy embodied in the Treaty.</p>
                <closer><signed>S[<hi rendition="#small-caps">umner</hi>] W[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">elles</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
